data:{"id":"1ef52082-f5b7-488b-b928-ef7d74ba5adf","response_type":"tool_result","content":"","done":false,"data":{"ambiguous_slugs":{"entity/chrome-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/freebsd":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/unix":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"duration_ms":12,"error":"","found_kbs":{"concept/command-line-interface":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/multi-tasking-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/network-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/open-source-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/unix-like-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/chrome-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/dos":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/freebsd":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux-mint":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/ubuntu":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/unix":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"output":"\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/dos|DOS]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e磁盘操作系统, Disk Operating System\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[concept/command-line-interface]] (命令行界面是一种基于文本的用户交互方式，用户通过在提示符下输入特定命令来执行程序和管理文件，区别于图形用户界面。)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"9286e345-411e-42bf-8083-02c97dac28b2\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nDOS is an early personal computer operating system that uses a text-based command-line interface, requiring users to type commands at a prompt like c:\\\u003e to manage files and run applications.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# DOS\n\nDOS (Disk Operating System, 磁盘操作系统) is one of the first major operating systems for personal computers. It served as the primary software layer that managed hardware resources and provided a platform for running applications during the initial era of personal computing.\n\nUnlike modern graphical user interfaces, DOS operated on a text-based paradigm. When a computer running DOS was powered on, the screen displayed a command prompt, typically appearing as `c:\\\u003e`, indicating the current drive and directory. This visual cue signaled that the system was ready to accept input.\n\n## User Interaction\n\nThe system relied entirely on a [[concept/command-line-interface|命令行界面]] (Command-Line Interface, or CLI). There were no menus to click or buttons to press. To perform any action, such as launching a program, navigating directories, or managing files, the user had to manually type specific instructions.\n\nFor example, to launch a word processing application, a user had to know the exact path and filename of the executable program. A typical command might look like `c:\\\u003ewp\\wp.exe`, where:\n*   `c:\\\u003e` is the prompt indicating readiness.\n*   `wp\\` specifies the subdirectory named \"wp\".\n*   `wp.exe` is the executable file name.\n\nThis requirement for precise syntax meant that users needed to memorize command structures and file paths. While this presented a steeper learning curve compared to later graphical systems, it offered a direct way to control the computer's operations.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/linux|Linux]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/unix]] (UNIX is a multi-tasking, command-line operating system that functions as both an independent and network OS across various hardware architectures.), [[entity/ubuntu]] (Ubuntu is a popular Linux distribution recommended for users seeking a non-Windows operating system for daily use on personal computers.), [[entity/linux-mint]] (Linux Mint is a popular Linux distribution recommended for personal computers as a user-friendly, stable, and secure alternative to proprietary operating systems.), [[entity/freebsd]] (FreeBSD is an open-source operating system that uses a different kernel than Linux but shares much of the same software ecosystem, offering a similar desktop experience.), [[entity/chrome-os]] (Chrome OS is an operating system mentioned as an alternative to Windows for personal computers, listed alongside Linux and FreeBSD in discussions of non-Windows computing platforms.)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (This document defines UNIX as a versatile multi-tasking command-line and network operating system, while introducing Linux as a popular, free UNIX-type alternative for various computing environments.), [[concept/unix-like-operating-system]] (A Unix-like operating system is a category of systems behaving similarly to Unix, including Linux and FreeBSD, which share similar software ecosystems despite kernel differences.), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (This page defines open-source operating systems as platforms with publicly accessible source code, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, as well as FreeBSD.), [[entity/ubuntu]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux-mint]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system]] (A multi-tasking operating system enables the concurrent execution of multiple processes or tasks, a capability found in systems like UNIX and Linux.), [[entity/freebsd]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nLinux is a popular, free, multi-tasking Unix-like operating system recommended for PC users seeking alternatives to Windows, with Ubuntu and Linux Mint being notable distributions.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Linux\n\nLinux is a popular, free, multi-tasking [[entity/unix|UNIX]]-like operating system widely used in computing environments. It is classified as a \"UNIX type\" operating system, meaning it adheres to the standards and behaviors established by UNIX, ensuring compatibility with many UNIX tools and methodologies. Like its predecessor, it is capable of running multiple applications and services concurrently.\n\nFor users seeking to install a non-Windows operating system on their personal computer for daily use, Linux is often the recommended choice. These systems provide a robust alternative to proprietary operating systems, offering stability, security, and a wide range of software support. Among the various options available, [[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]] and [[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]] stand out as the most popular distributions. The popularity of these distributions stems from their user-friendly interfaces and extensive community support, making them ideal candidates for those transitioning away from Windows.\n\nWhile Linux dominates the open-source desktop landscape, there are other notable open-source operating systems, such as [[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]]. Although FreeBSD utilizes a different kernel compared to Linux, the user experience on a desktop PC is remarkably similar. This similarity arises because FreeBSD shares much of the same software ecosystem found in typical Linux distributions. Users familiar with Linux will find the transition to FreeBSD relatively smooth, as the applications and tools they rely on are largely compatible or have direct equivalents. [[entity/chrome-os|Chrome OS]] is also mentioned as an alternative operating system in the broader discussion of non-Windows platforms.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/unix|UNIX]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/network-operating-system]] (A Network Operating System supports workstations, personal computers, and terminals on a local area network. UNIX is cited as an example that functions as both an independent and network operating system.)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/network-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nUNIX is a multi-tasking, command-line operating system that functions as both an independent and network OS across various hardware architectures.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# UNIX\n\nUNIX is a robust operating system designed to function across a wide variety of computer hardware architectures. It is fundamentally characterized as a [[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system|Multi-tasking Operating System]], allowing it to handle multiple processes simultaneously. Its primary interface for user interaction and system management is through a [[concept/command-line-interface|Command-line Interface]] (CLI), which provides powerful control over system resources and operations.\n\nThe unique architecture of UNIX allows it to serve dual roles in computing infrastructure. It operates effectively as a standalone, independent operating system for individual machines, while also possessing the capabilities of a [[concept/network-operating-system|Network Operating System]] (NOS). This dual nature enables it to manage network resources, facilitate communication between different systems, and support distributed computing environments. Due to this flexibility, UNIX is frequently referred to as a \"multi-purpose operating system.\"\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[concept/unix-like-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nFreeBSD is an open-source operating system that uses a different kernel than Linux but shares much of the same software ecosystem, offering a similar desktop experience.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# FreeBSD\n\nFreeBSD is an open-source operating system. Although it utilizes a different kernel compared to [[entity/linux|Linux]], the user experience on a desktop PC is remarkably similar. This similarity arises because FreeBSD shares much of the same software ecosystem found in typical Linux distributions. Users familiar with Linux will find the transition to FreeBSD relatively smooth, as the applications and tools they rely on are largely compatible or have direct equivalents.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/chrome-os|Chrome OS]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e铬操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e(none)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nChrome OS is an operating system mentioned as an alternative to Windows for personal computers, listed alongside Linux and FreeBSD in discussions of non-Windows computing platforms.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Chrome OS\n\nChrome OS (referred to as \"铬操作系统\" in some sources) is an operating system mentioned in the context of alternative PC operating systems. It is listed alongside other major non-Windows operating systems, indicating its relevance in the broader discussion of alternative computing platforms.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e","success":true,"tool_call_id":"call_45afd9fecc804ff9a470940b","tool_name":"wiki_read_page"}}

